{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CghuCMBZ9haos5r4UXgRH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XgGTyvrxT3CnewrCrHx99F","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-build--20261104-053803--x4cdb93-work/9e576ea7-ae6c-4602-b536-6bfa64a9b891/tool-results/bttqiyxal.txt","limit":400},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1063,"cache_read_input_tokens":22182,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1063},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9e576ea7-ae6c-4602-b536-6bfa64a9b891","uuid":"704e4c16-cf03-4c72-9e4f-5d2a973bc8ba","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:38:10.601Z","request_id":"req_011CghuCLuw1T43P1N1eVSAo","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01XgGTyvrxT3CnewrCrHx99F":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-build--20261104-053803--x4cdb93-work/9e576ea7-ae6c-4602-b536-6bfa64a9b891/tool-results/bttqiyxal.txt","limit":400}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XgGTyvrxT3CnewrCrHx99F","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Big Buck Hunt-Off\n2\t\n3\tBuild plan for the developers who will make this game from `TASK.md` and this file alone. Read `TASK.md` first, then this\n4\tplan top to bottom once; after that, use it as a reference. Everything here is a decision unless it says \"optional\".\n5\tWhere a number is given, it is the starting default (a dev slider where marked), to be tuned in play.\n6\t\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t## 0. At a glance\n10\t\n11\t| | |\n12\t|---|---|\n13\t| **Title** | **Big Buck Hunt-Off** (on screen: `BIG BUCK HUNT-OFF`). Picked as TASK.md asks: 50 riffs on \"Big Buck Hunter Pro\" brainstormed, one drawn with Node's `crypto.randomInt` (it drew #33 of 50). |\n14\t| **Dev subfolder** | `BigBuckHuntOff-BigBuckHunterPro-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174813/` (GameTitle and SOURCE in PascalCase with spaces and punctuation dropped) |\n15\t| **Deliverable** | `BigBuckHuntOff-BigBuckHunterPro-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174813.html` in this folder's root (beside `PLAN.md`), plus `exit-interview.json` beside it |\n16\t| **Genre** | Arcade light-gun hunting shooter (a shooting gallery in a living landscape), with party versus and bonus mini-games |\n17\t| **Perspective** | First person, on rails: a fixed vantage per site with scripted camera pans over a pseudo-3D heightfield landscape. Plus a slow-motion bullet cam on trophy kills, side-on x-ray Shot Chart cards in the tallies, and 2D screens for menus and the Lodge |\n18\t| **Players** | 1 to 4 on one screen (simultaneous, competitive); 1 to 4 online in AI Hub sessions (ghost mode) |\n19\t| **Engine** | my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff package (full engine `my-3d2dge.js`), extended with new modules |\n20\t\n21\t**Pitch.** It's the 41st Annual Big Buck Hunt-Off at Whisker Lake Lodge. You sit in a tree stand, a blind or a canoe and\n22\tlook out over a landscape that lives: deer, elk and moose wander, graze, stop and bolt across creek fords, cornfields,\n23\talpine meadows and frozen bogs. Pick out the bucks (only the bucks), pump your rifle between shots, and go for clean,\n24\tlong, running shots for big points. Shoot a doe and you sit out the rest of the site. After each site an x-ray Shot\n25\tChart shows exactly where every round went. Each hunt ends with a legendary trophy animal and a silly bonus round, and\n26\tyour total climbs the Hunt-Off standings against the lodge regulars. Friends can play on the same couch with their own\n27\treticles, or online through AI Hub.\n28\t\n29\t**Core loop (one sentence, put it at the top of the game code):** spot the bucks, shoot clean, pump, never shoot a doe;\n30\tbag them before they're gone, then read your shots in x-ray and climb the standings.\n31\t\n32\t**Session lengths:** Hunt-Off campaign about 17 minutes (3 hunts); a single hunt about 6 minutes; Party Quick Draw\n33\tabout 5 minutes; an AI Hub round 3 minutes; Practice Range about 3 minutes.\n34\t\n35\t### 0.1 Where each TASK.md requirement is covered\n36\t\n37\tThis stage writes the plan only (STAGE.md: \"Don't build it yet: plan it\"). The game's HTML and `exit-interview.json`\n38\tare build-stage outputs; this plan names them, specifies them and gives the exit interview's filled template.\n39\t\n40\t| TASK.md asks for | Covered in |\n41\t|---|---|\n42\t| Genre(s) that fit the source | 0 (genre), 1.2, 2 |\n43\t| Camera perspective(s) | 0 (perspective), 5.1, 5.13 (bullet cam), 5.14 (Shot Chart), 5.18 (Lodge) |\n44\t| Title: a riff on the source's name, 50 brainstormed, one picked by a real RNG | 0 (Big Buck Hunt-Off, #33 of 50 by `crypto.randomInt`) |\n45\t| Research (our choice) | 1.1 (each fact confirmed, reported or unknown), Appendix F |\n46\t| Package: one HTML file with the exact name; downloadable dependencies optional | 0, 3.5 (bundle layout, build checks), 10 |\n47\t| Tech stack on the handoff package; the engine may change; new systems, art, animation | 3.1, 4.1, Appendix C (engine changes) |\n48\t| Art direction: engine's native style, new art for the source's feel, library for generic props | 2, 5.3 (steps 4-5), 5.4, 5.5 |\n49\t| Animation: engine's native system, new animations | 5.4 (Quadruped gaits and poses), 5.5 (Humanoid and Blob poses) |\n50\t| Audio: music, sfx and voices in code; gibberish voices | 5.19 (E.Babble), 5.20 (songs, ambience, sfx) |\n51\t| Online: AI Hub lib/hub | 5.26 (ghost mode), 3.5 (metas, client first), 9.4 (harness) |\n52\t| Working environment: dev subfolder, modules then one bundle in root, CDN allowed, shared machine etiquette, `file://`, server on 127.0.0.1 port 0, stop only own PIDs | 0, 3.2, 3.3, 3.5, 9.4 |\n53\t| Performance budget unlimited (FPS out of scope) | 5.3 (detail, effects, second render pass for scopes), 8 (renderer row) |\n54\t| Testing: headless scripts with `game.advance` and `game.debug`, a scripted playthrough of the whole game, one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changes, screenshots only where scripts can't check, no subagent or workflow reviews | 9.1-9.5, the Tests paragraph of each system in 5, Appendix D (debug handle) |\n55\t| Goal: an original game inspired by the source | 1.2, 1.3, 1.4 |\n56\t| Scope: feature-complete, polished vertical slice showing all features; world, characters, themes, tone; infrastructure to build the rest with designer tools | 5 (all systems), 6 (all content), 4.2 (registries and designer tools), 7 (M6 slice first) |\n57\t| Dev mode from the pause menu: standard tools, live sliders for key variables, reset to defaults | 5.22, Appendix A, Appendix C (3) |\n58\t| Attract mode: 10 s idle on title or menu, AI demo loop, any input exits, may cheat | 5.21, 9.2 (flow/attract) |\n59\t| Multiplayer: AI Hub integration and the game on its own | 5.11 (local 1-4), 5.26 (hub; runs normally when `connect()` answers null) |\n60\t| Narrative tone like the source | 2, 6.8 (story beats), 6.9 (barks) |\n61\t| 10-foot, controller-first UI; DualSense or any standard pad; KB+M native with the genre's keys; mouse aims, clicks and hover-focuses; prompts follow the last device; PlayStation glyphs with an Xbox option; no dense web-style screens | 5.12, 5.17, 5.23, 5.24, Appendix C (2) |\n62\t| Touch-only devices: no touch controls, \"Controller or keyboard required\", the settings button, attract mode runs | 5.25 |\n63\t| Done: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs | 3.5 (build check), 9.2 (unit/lint), 10 |\n64\t| Source Information (every field, \"unknown\" when unconfirmed) in the chat reply and an About & Credits screen | 5.27, Appendix E.1 |\n65\t| Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback answers | 5.27, Appendix E.2 |\n66\t| `exit-interview.json` in the exact shape, beside the HTML, checked to parse | Appendix E.3 (filled template, validated) |\n67\t\n68\t---\n69\t\n70\t## 1. The source: what to capture, what not to copy\n71\t\n72\t### 1.1 What research confirmed about Big Buck Hunter Pro\n73\t\n74\tResearch done while planning, on 2026-11-03: pages read directly were Wikipedia's \"Big Buck Hunter\" article, the\n75\tHighway Games product listing for the Big Buck Hunter Pro cabinet, and Macworld's review of the iPhone port. Giant\n76\tBomb, TV Tropes, MobyGames and the Sinden wiki refused the fetch (HTTP 403) and one retailer page was rate-limited\n77\t(HTTP 429); for those, only search-result summaries were seen. **Confirmed** below means read on a fetched page;\n78\t**reported** means seen only in search summaries; **unknown** means not found. Use \"unknown\" in the reports for\n79\tanything not confirmed.\n80\t\n81\t| Fact | Status | Where |\n82\t|---|---|---|\n83\t| Arcade game designed by Play Mechanix, produced and distributed by Raw Thrills; released January 23, 2006 (a retailer database lists 2005) | confirmed | Wikipedia; Highway Games |\n84\t| Two guns on one cabinet; 1-2 players; single, versus and co-operative play, plus a head-to-head \"Shootout\" mode | confirmed | Wikipedia; Highway Games |\n85\t| Shoot the males as they cross the screen; a player who shoots a female is out (the iPhone port: hitting a doe ends the scene, bucks already taken still count) | confirmed | Wikipedia; Macworld |\n86\t| A hunt is split into five screens called treks, and a bonus round ends the session; a round ends when all males are shot or some escape | confirmed | Wikipedia |\n87\t| Points depend on distance, the animal's weight or size, rack points, accuracy and bonus critters | confirmed | Wikipedia; Macworld |\n88\t| A buck dies to one shot in the vitals or two anywhere on the body; after each scene a screen shows where the shots landed (the iPhone port) | confirmed | Macworld |\n89\t| Animals graze until a few seconds pass or the first shot, then bolt; each scene has three bucks, often with a doe (the iPhone port) | confirmed | Macworld |\n90\t| Five huntable animals: whitetail deer, elk, pronghorn antelope, moose, bighorn sheep; hunts organized by species, not region | confirmed | Highway Games |\n91\t| Critters for bonus points: mountain lions, raccoons, possums, skunks, blackbirds, bluebirds, squirrels, foxes, wolves, rabbits | confirmed | Highway Games |\n92\t| 15 bonus rounds, also sold as \"Bonus Only\" (named: Duck Hunt, Pappy's Moonshine, Pie in the Sky, Boars Gone Wild, Mug Shot, Turkey Hunt) | confirmed | Highway Games |\n93\t| Monthly national tournaments | confirmed | Wikipedia |\n94\t| Pump-action guns (\"pump to reload\" speech call) | reported | search summary of a Big Buck Hunter Reloaded change log; the iPhone port reloads after every shot (Macworld, confirmed) |\n95\t| Head shots, heart shots, or \"peppering\" a buck | reported | search summary of Giant Bomb |\n96\t| Tone: playful, jokey (the review's \"Freeze, gopher!\"), rural humor in the bonus round names | confirmed in spirit | Macworld; Highway Games |\n97\t| The announcer's exact lines, the cabinet's art and music | unknown | (not researched on purpose: we don't copy them) |\n98\t\n99\t### 1.2 Distinctive elements, turned into our mechanics\n100\t\n101\t| Source element | Our version (original) |\n102\t|---|---|\n103\t| Two pump guns, head to head | Up to 4 simultaneous reticles, each with its own pump-action rifle; shared animals, first hit takes it |\n104\t| Bucks only, a doe puts you out | Bucks/bulls are targets; does, fawns and calves are protected. Classic rule: shoot one and you're benched for the site (a Casual setting turns it into a points penalty) |\n105\t| Sites (treks) and a bonus round | 3 hunts, each with 3 sites, a legendary **Trophy** encounter and a bonus game, all on one landscape per hunt with walk-in camera moves between vantages |\n106\t| Distance, weight and accuracy scoring | Points = species base (from antler size) × distance × hit zone × running bonus, plus accuracy, clean sweep and critter bonuses |\n107\t| Head and heart shots; a screen showing where shots landed | Real 3D hit zones on every animal (heart, lungs, head, neck, shoulder, gut, ham, legs, antlers), shown afterwards on our own **x-ray Shot Chart** |\n108\t| Critters for bonus points | 12 critter designs across the hunts; some protected (skunk, Bessie the cow, eagle, loons) with comic penalties |\n109\t| Goofy bonus rounds | Crow Patrol, Saucer Season, Snowball Standoff (ours) |\n110\t| Tournaments | The Hunt-Off campaign against rival regulars; AI Hub rounds as an online Hunt-Off |\n111\t| Pump to reload | Pump (rack) after every shot; when the tube is empty, the pump reloads it |\n112\t| Loud announcer | An emcee with short all-caps barks, plus a folksy guide and rivals who talk in Animalese gibberish with captions |\n113\t\n114\t### 1.3 Additions that make it a better game\n115\t- **The call** (grunt, bugle, moose grunt): a limited button that makes nearby animals stop and look for a moment: a\n116\t  skill shot setup, and the key to two trophies.\n117\t- **The scope**: a round magnifying lens around your reticle (works for four players on one screen), with gentle sway\n118\t  and a hold-breath button for long shots across a valley.\n119\t- **Hills, cover and light**: animals vanish behind ridges, trunks and fog banks; dawn mist, dusk backlight, snowfall.\n120\t\n121\t### 1.4 Do not copy\n122\tNo names, logos, characters, art, music, sounds, bonus-round concepts, levels or look from Big Buck Hunter. Our title,\n123\tcast, hunting grounds, bonus games, writing and art are our own. Whitetail deer, elk and moose are real animals every\n124\thunting game shares (the source also had pronghorn and bighorn sheep); our hunts, sites, trophies and critter roles\n125\tare original, and none of our bonus games matches the source's named rounds. Reticle colors are the AI Hub seat colors (red, blue,\n126\tgreen, gold), not the cabinet's gun colors. No gore: hits throw puffs of fur and dust; animals fold up and lie down.\n127\t\n128\t---\n129\t\n130\t## 2. Look, sound and tone\n131\t\n132\t**Art direction: the engine's native retro-modern pixel style** (its Look target: PS1/N64-era 2D, shaded with\n133\t`E.tones`, outlined pixel art, the 5x7 font, `E.font.title` logos). Internal resolution `res: [480, 270]` with\n134\t`expand: true` (a 16:9 screen shows 480 x 270; wider screens show more landscape). The world is drawn by our own\n135\tpseudo-3D renderer (section 5.3) in the engine's pixels: a heightfield landscape a column at a time like the early-90s\n136\tvoxel terrain games, with billboards (trees, rocks, animals) scaled by distance.\n137\t\n138\tNew art made for this game: every animal and critter (procedural rigs), antlers, hunting props (tree stand, ground\n139\tblind frame, canoe bow, paper targets, trap house, scarecrow Hank, snowmen, farm dressing: barn, porch, corn rows),\n140\tthe Lodge, the logo, glyph icons, HUD pieces. Generic props come from the engine's library and asset store\n141\t(pines, broadleaf trees, bushes, rocks, stumps, logs, fences, hay bales, tents, signposts, campfire rings: 5.3,\n142\tstep 4).\n143\t\n144\tPer-hunt palettes:\n145\t- **Hollow Creek** (autumn morning): pale blue sky to peach horizon, low eastern sun, olive and ochre grass, russet\n146\t  leaf litter, orange oaks, red maples, white birches, dark teal creek with white riffles, valley mist.\n147\t- **Bugle Ridge** (alpine dusk): deep blue to orange-pink sky, sun setting behind the peaks (backlit animals with warm\n148\t  rim outlines), gold aspens, dark spruce, grey talus, gold-green meadow, a lake below catching the sunset.\n149\t- **Muskeg Lake** (first snow, overcast): grey-blue sky, falling snow, white and blue-grey ground, black spruce, orange\n150\t  tamaracks, brown reeds and cattails, dark water with ice at the edges, dense far fog.\n151\t\n152\t**Tone and writing**: like the source, short, loud and folksy. The emcee barks in all caps (\"BUCKS ONLY!\", \"HEART\n153\tSHOT!\", \"DOE! YOU'RE OUT!\"); the guide is dry and homespun; the rivals are small-town braggarts. Lines stay under\n154\tabout 60 characters so they read at 10 feet. Sample lines are in 6.8 and 6.9; write the rest in the same voice.\n155\t\n156\t**Audio**: everything synthesized in code (engine chip synth plus small Web Audio graphs). Bluegrass-flavored chiptune\n157\tmusic (banjo-roll pulse arpeggios, fiddle square leads, triangle bass, washboard noise drums), nature ambience, layered\n158\tgunshots with valley echoes, animal calls, Animalese/Simlish gibberish voices. Section 5.20.\n159\t\n160\t---\n161\t\n162\t## 3. Setup, folders and build\n163\t\n164\t### 3.1 Getting the engine and the hub client\n165\tThe plan was written against **engine handoff v0.85.2 (my-3d2dge commit 4390a98)** and **AI Hub lib/hub (ai-hub commit\n166\tf8835e4, session client 1.4.0)**. A copy fetched while planning is at\n167\t`/home/micha/.cache/x2e02c0-plan-research/{engine,hub}` (sparse clones: `engine/dist/handoff/`, `hub/lib/hub/`). Copy\n168\tfrom it, or clone again the same way into a new, empty folder inside the dev subfolder:\n169\t\n170\t```sh\n171\tgit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git vendor/engine-src\n172\tgit -C vendor/engine-src sparse-checkout set dist/handoff\n173\tgit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub.git vendor/hub-src\n174\tgit -C vendor/hub-src sparse-checkout set lib/hub\n175\t```\n176\t\n177\tRead in the handoff: `README.md`, `API.md` (whole), `AI_GUIDE.md` (as needed), `docs/ASSETS.md` (whole, before\n178\tdrawing props), `API-INDEX.md` (grep only), `modules/mode7.js` header (our renderer borrows its view cache and its\n179\tBackdrop-turning trick), `modules/arcade.js` header, `kits/kart.js` (Mode 7 usage), `game/` (template and its test).\n180\tIn the hub: `README.md` (whole), `examples/ghost.html` (the pattern we follow), `harness.html`.\n181\t\n182\t### 3.2 Environment notes (this machine)\n183\t- `/tmp` is a nearly full tmpfs (99-100% used by others). Point scratch space at the dev folder: `export\n184\t  TMPDIR=$PWD/.tmp` before Playwright, Chromium or anything that writes temp files. Never delete other people's files.\n185\t- Node 24 is installed; Playwright's browsers are cached in `~/.cache/ms-playwright` (chromium-1243). `npm install`\n186\t  in `vendor/engine-src/dist/handoff` installs the pinned Playwright 1.63.0 for the engine's tools (if it wants a\n187\t  different Chromium build than the cache has, run `npx playwright install chromium` there).\n188\t- Other agents share this machine, its ports and processes. Prefer `file://` and headless Node. The hub harness needs a\n189\t  server: `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` (it prints the port), record its PID, check the page title is\n190\t  ours before trusting a result, and stop only that PID.\n191\t\n192\t### 3.3 Dev folder layout\n193\t\n194\t```\n195\tBigBuckHuntOff-BigBuckHunterPro-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174813/\n196\t  engine/my-3d2dge.js           the full engine, our copy (changes listed in Appendix C)\n197\t  engine/modules/vista.js       NEW engine module: E.Lens, E.Heightfield, E.Vista (renderer), E.Impostors\n198\t  engine/modules/quadruped.js   NEW engine module: E.Quadruped (four-legged rig) and antler generators\n199\t  engine/modules/babble.js      NEW engine module: E.Babble (gibberish voices) and audio echo helper\n200\t  engine/assets/nature.js       the few store entries we use, cut with tools/asset-set.mjs\n201\t  hub/session.js                AI Hub client 1.4.0, verbatim (never edited)\n202\t  hub/harness.html bridge.js round.js   for the conformance check only (not bundled)\n203\t  src/*.js                      the game, in numbered files (3.4)\n204\t  build/manifest.json           the script order, shared by the build and the tests\n205\t  build/build.mjs               bundles everything into the single HTML in the work root\n206\t  index.html                    the dev page: loads the same scripts as separate files\n207\t  tests/run.mjs                 THE test command (section 9)\n208\t  tests/lib/*.mjs               the headless loader, the hub stub, the bot driver, Playwright helpers\n209\t  tests/unit/*.test.mjs, tests/flow/*.test.mjs, tests/browser/*.test.mjs\n210\t  tools/                        copies of the engine's check.mjs, filmstrip.mjs, replay.mjs (paths fixed up)\n211\t  .tmp/                         scratch (gitignore-style: never bundled)\n212\t```\n213\t\n214\t### 3.4 Game source files (order = load order)\n215\t\n216\t| File | Holds |\n217\t|---|---|\n218\t| `00-core.js` | `const E = My3D2dge`; constants (`M = 16` world units per metre); `def(kind, id, spec)` registries; event bus; RNG streams; `TUNING` (dev settings); `Records` (E.store); the core-loop comment |\n219\t| `01-game.js` | `new E.Game(...)` (options in 5.24), our `E.Shell` with theme, help and game settings, E.Arcade instance |\n220\t| `10-world.js` | World: loads a hunt's Heightfield, props, water, sky, weather; caches up to 2 hunts |\n221\t| `11-props.js` | prop registry, custom prop painters, scatter rules, occluder volumes and their spatial grid |\n222\t| `12-fx.js` | world-space particles, screen-space weather, banners' shake; cosmetic RNG only |\n223\t| `20-species.js` | species presets for E.Quadruped (whitetail, elk, moose, canid, cow), palettes, antler specs |\n224\t| `21-critters.js` | 12 critter designs: rigs (E.Blob or E.Quadruped presets), behaviors, points, penalties |\n225\t| `22-cast.js` | Humanoid cast (Dub, Granny Mae, Dwayne, Loretta, Earl, the Emcee) and their voices |\n226\t| `30-actors.js` | animal and critter runtime: orders, paths on terrain, herds, senses, reactions, wounds, death |\n227\t| `31-patterns.js` | spawn pattern library (cross, emerge, graze, herdPass, ridgeRun, wade, bolt, charge, decoyDoe) |\n228\t| `32-site.js` | Site runtime: script runner, camera director, end rules, per-site results |\n229\t| `40-weapon.js` | rifle (pump, tube, reload), scope (lens, sway, breath), call |\n230\t| `41-aim.js` | reticle control per device, aim assist |\n231\t| `42-shot.js` | shot resolution (rays against zones, occluders, terrain, water), impacts, spook events |\n232\t| `43-score.js` | scoring, bonuses, penalties, stats, stars, ranks, rivals |\n233\t| `50-players.js` | player slots: device, color and reticle shape, weapon, score, benching; joining |\n234\t| `51-hud.js` | HUD, banners, score pops, captions, prompts |\n235\t| `52-glyphs.js` | PlayStation, Xbox, keyboard and mouse glyph icons; prompt labels by last device |\n236\t| `53-ui.js` | UIKit: focus lists and grids, cards, mouse hover focus, clicks, back, UI sounds |\n237\t| `60-tally.js` | site tally and x-ray Shot Chart |\n238\t| `61-trophy.js` | trophy encounters and the Trophy Cam |\n239\t| `62-bonus.js` | bonus game framework and the three bonus games |\n240\t| `63-range.js` | Practice Range stations |\n241\t| `70-audio.js` | songs, sfx definitions, ambience beds, gunshot echoes |\n242\t| `71-barks.js` | barks and captions; dialog scenes with portraits and Babble voices |\n243\t| `80-hollow.js`, `81-bugle.js`, `82-muskeg.js`, `83-range-map.js` | each hunt's map, vantages, sites, trophy, bonus |\n244\t| `85-story.js` | story beats, rivals' scores, ceremony |\n245\t| `90-title.js`, `91-menu.js`, `92-hunt.js`, `93-lodge.js`, `94-about.js` | scenes |\n246\t| `95-attract.js` | attract director and HunterBot |\n247\t| `96-dev.js` | dev mode: tuning rows, tools, debug overlays, free camera, Animal Lab, Sound Lab |\n248\t| `97-hub.js` | AI Hub ghost-mode integration |\n249\t| `99-start.js` | `game.debug`, `game.stateHash`, deep links, boot steps, `game.start` |\n250\t\n251\t### 3.5 The bundle\n252\t`node build/build.mjs` writes `../BigBuckHuntOff-BigBuckHunterPro-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174813.html`:\n253\t\n254\t```html\n255\t<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head>\n256\t<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n257\t<script>/* hub/session.js, byte for byte */</script>\n258\t<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\">\n259\t<title>Big Buck Hunt-Off</title>\n260\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n261\t<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n262\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n263\t<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"3\">\n264\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Most points in 3 minutes wins. Bucks only, never does!\">\n265\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Aim: mouse or left stick · Shoot: click or R2 · Pump: R or Square\">\n266\t<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\"><meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1-4\">\n267\t<style>html,body{margin:0;height:100%;background:#000;overflow:hidden}#screen{width:100%;height:100%;display:block;image-rendering:pixelated;touch-action:none;cursor:none}</style>\n268\t</head><body>\n269\t<!-- E.bootSplash('BIG BUCK HUNT-OFF') markup -->\n270\t<canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas>\n271\t<script>/* engine/my-3d2dge.js */</script>\n272\t<script>/* engine/modules/vista.js, quadruped.js, babble.js; engine/assets/nature.js */</script>\n273\t<script>/* src/*.js in manifest order, each in its own <script> or concatenated inside one IIFE per file */</script>\n274\t</body></html>\n275\t```\n276\t\n277\tThe build asserts: the session client is the first script right after `<meta charset>` and matches `hub/session.js`\n278\texactly (hash); every hub meta is present; goal is at most 60 characters and howto at most 80; no `TODO`, `FIXME`,\n279\t`XXX`, `placeholder` or `lorem` anywhere in `src/` or the new modules; no `fetch(`, `WebSocket`, `RTCPeerConnection`,\n280\t`alert(`, `confirm(`, `prompt(` or `window.open` in game code. It runs from `file://` with no network. No CDN is needed.\n281\t\n282\t---\n283\t\n284\t## 4. Architecture\n285\t\n286\t### 4.1 The stack\n287\t\n288\t```\n289\t AI Hub client (session.js)  ─ first script; inert outside a session\n290\t my-3D2dge full engine       ─ game loop (fixed 1/120 s steps), scenes, input (keys, mouse, pads, players),\n291\t                               standard menus (E.Shell HTML: pause, settings, controls, help), settings, saves,\n292\t                               E.store, audio synth + music clock, E.Arcade (tables, initials), E.Dialog,\n293\t                               E.Humanoid, E.Blob, E.Backdrop, E.View, px/font/ui drawing, tweens, scripts, paths\n294\t NEW engine modules          ─ E.Lens, E.Heightfield, E.Vista, E.Impostors   (vista.js)\n295\t                               E.Quadruped, E.Antlers                         (quadruped.js)\n296\t                               E.Babble, audio echo send                      (babble.js)\n297\t Game (src/)                 ─ content registries, actors and AI, sites, weapons, shots, scoring, players,\n298\t                               HUD/UI, modes and scenes, attract, dev mode, hub link\n299\t```\n300\t\n301\tWe do not use `E.Mode7` itself (its floor is flat); `vista.js` reuses its ideas: `view(k)` caching for drawing rigs at a\n302\tdistance's scale, and drawing a Backdrop through a stand-in renderer whose `ix` turns with the heading.\n303\t\n304\t### 4.2 Conventions\n305\t- **Units**: 16 world units = 1 m (`M = 16`). x east, y south, z up (the engine's). Content data is written in\n306\t  metres and degrees and converted on load.\n307\t- **Angles**: yaw 0 = east (+x), pi/2 = south (+y). Site data uses bearings in degrees relative to the site's base\n308\t  yaw (negative = left of centre) and distances in metres: `[bearing, dist]`.\n309\t- **Time**: everything in seconds of game time; `update(dt)` only (fixed steps). Gameplay never reads `performance.now`.\n310\t- **Randomness**: never `Math.random` in gameplay. `RNG.stream(name)` returns `E.rng(seed)` generators: `world`\n311\t  (map generation: seed per hunt, always the same map), `spawn` (site variations: seed per run), `ai` (per animal,\n312\t  seeded from the run seed and the animal's id), `bot`, and `fx` (cosmetic only; may be drawn from freely, so its\n313\t  draws never shift the others). In an AI Hub session the run seed comes from `AIHub.rng('hunt')`. A test greps `src/`\n314\t  and the new modules for `Math.random` (there should be none).\n315\t- **Registries** (`def(kind, id, spec)`): `species`, `critter`, `hunt`, `site`, `trophy`, `bonus`, `pattern`, `prop`,\n316\t  `bark`, `voice`, `song`, `beat` (story). Content refers to other content by id and never edits core code. This is\n317\t  what lets the rest of the game be built out later with designer tools: a new hunt, site, species, critter or bonus\n318\t  game is data (plus at most a small painter), authored with the dev free camera's position and bearing readout and\n319\t  previewed in the Animal Lab and Sound Lab.\n320\t- **Event bus**: `bus.on(name, fn)`, `bus.emit(name, data)`. Events: `shot`, `hit`, `kill`, `wound`, `miss`, `doe`,\n321\t  `critter`, `penalty`, `spook`, `escape`, `lost`, `siteStart`, `siteEnd`, `trophySpotted`, `bark`, `pump`, `reload`,\n322\t  `call`. HUD, barks, audio, stats, the bot and the hub link listen; gameplay code emits.\n323\t- **Determinism**: the same seed and inputs give the same game (the engine's fixed step plus our streams).\n324\t  `game.stateHash = () => [every actor's x, y, state, hp, every player's score, rounds]`.\n325\t\n326\t### 4.3 Scenes and flow\n327\t\n328\tEngine scenes (`game.go(name, data, { fade })`):\n329\t\n330\t| Scene | pausable | What |\n331\t|---|---|---|\n332\t| `title` | no | live Hollow Creek vista behind the logo; PRESS START; idle 10 s starts the attract mode (5.21) |\n333\t| `attract` | no | the demo playlist; any input returns to `title` |\n334\t| `menu` | no | main menu and its sub-pages (one scene, a page stack): Hunt-Off, Single Hunt, Party Hunt, Bonus Games, Practice Range, The Lodge, Settings, About & Credits. Idle 10 s on a top-level page starts the attract mode too |\n335\t| `hunt` | **yes** | all gameplay: a hunt's segments (below), the Range, bonus-only and party modes |\n336\t| `lodge` | no | the trophy room (side view, engine props and lights) |\n337\t| `about` | no | About & Credits pages (5.27) |\n338\t| `hub` | no (pausing is off in sessions) | the AI Hub round: own card, the timed Hub Hunt-Off, final standings |\n339\t| `animallab`, `soundlab` | no | dev-only labs (reachable from dev mode and deep links) |\n340\t\n341\tSegments inside `hunt` (one scene, so the loaded world, timers and particles survive between them):\n342\t\n343\t```\n344\thuntIntro (establishing pan, hunt card, briefing dialog)\n345\t  -> site 1: siteIntro -> play -> siteEnd -> tally -> walk (camera walks the trail to the next vantage)\n346\t  -> site 2 ...  -> site 3 ...\n347\t  -> trophyBrief (campfire legend dialog) -> trophy (play) -> trophyEnd (Trophy Cam / \"the one that got away\") -> tally\n348\t  -> bonusBrief (host dialog) -> bonus (play) -> bonusTally\n349\t  -> huntResults (total, rank, rivals, initials via E.Arcade) -> outro dialog\n350\t  -> next hunt (Hunt-Off campaign: autosave, then load the next world) or back to the menu\n351\t```\n352\t\n353\tPause menu items (`menu: { items }`): Restart site, Restart hunt, Dev mode. Standard: Resume, Settings, Controls, Full\n354\tscreen, Help, Quit to title (`quit: 'title'`).\n355\t\n356\t### 4.4 The frame\n357\t\n358\t`update(dt)` (each fixed step), in this order: input and reticles -> weapons (pump timers, scope, breath, calls) ->\n359\tshots fired this step are resolved -> site script and spawns -> actors (AI, movement, gaits, rigs) -> director camera ->\n360\tfx -> HUD state, barks, audio cues -> hub link (session only).\n361\t\n362\t`draw(r)`: `vista.draw(r, lens)` as one `r.overlay`: sky and Backdrop, terrain raster, billboards far to near (props,\n363\tanimals, critters, world particles), foreground dressing, weather, fog and grade, then the scope lenses, reticles,\n364\tscore pops; then the HUD overlay. Engine lighting, the engine camera and engine particles are not used in Vista scenes\n365\t(the overlay is unlit and screen-space); the Lodge uses the engine's normal side view, props and lights.\n366\t\n367\t### 4.5 The test handle\n368\t`game.debug` (Appendix D): `snapshot()`, `aimAt(p, actorId, zone)`, `fire(p)`, `pump(p)`, `scope(p, on)`,\n369\t`call(p)`, `goto(huntId, segment)`, `spawn(kind, opts)`, `skip()`, `bagAll()`, `bot(on, opts)`, `seed(n)`,\n370\t`tuning(key, value)`. Tests, `tools/check.mjs` and the bot use it.\n371\t\n372\t---\n373\t\n374\t## 5. Systems\n375\t\n376\tEach system lists what to build, its numbers and how to test it (test names refer to section 9).\n377\t\n378\t### 5.1 Lens and the camera director (`vista.js`: `E.Lens`; `32-site.js`: Director)\n379\t\n380\t**Projection** (a sheared perspective: verticals stay vertical, which suits billboards and the column renderer):\n381\t- Camera `{ x, y, z, yaw, pitch, fov }` (fov = degrees across the screen's height; pitch + = looking down).\n382\t- Screen `W x H`, centre `cx = W/2`, `cy = H/2`; focal `f = (H/2) / tan(fov/2)`; `horizonY = cy - f * tan(pitch)`.\n383\t- Forward `F = (cos yaw, sin yaw)`, right `R = (-sin yaw, cos yaw)`.\n384\t- `project(P)`: `d = P - C`; `D = d.F` (forward distance); `L = d.R`; null if `D < 1 m`; `k = f / D` (pixels per unit);\n385\t  `sx = cx + L*k`; `sy = horizonY - (P.z - C.z)*k`. Returns `[sx, sy, k, D]`.\n386\t- `ray(sx, sy)`: direction `dir = F + R*((sx - cx)/f) + up*((horizonY - sy)/f)`; the point at forward distance D is\n387\t  `C + D*dir`. Shots, picking and the bot all use this.\n388\t- **Magnifying about a point** (the scope lens): the same camera with focal `f*mag`, centred so screen point `s0` lands\n389\t  on the lens centre: `s' = c_lens + (s - s0)*mag`. The lens is an exact zoom of the main view, so the shot through its\n390\t  centre is the main view's ray through `s0`.\n391\t- **Rigs and props** are drawn as orthographic billboards: an `E.View('vista', 'Vista', yawDeg, pitchDeg, k, 1)` with\n392\t  `yawDeg = -yaw*180/pi - 90` (Mode 7's formula) and `pitchDeg` = the angle from the camera down to the object,\n393\t  clamped to 4..30 degrees, quantized to 2 degrees; views cached by (k in 1/32 steps below 1, 1/16 above; yaw in 2\n394\t  degree steps; pitch).\n395\t\n396\t**Director** (per site, deterministic; keyed to site time, never to player input, so all players and all AI Hub copies\n397\tsee the same frame): keyframes `[t, { bearing, pitch, fov, ease, dur }]`; optional `follow` mode in single player\n398\t(yaw eases toward the centroid of live bucks, clamped to the site's bearing range, smoothed over 0.8 s). Single player\n399\talso gets a 0.4 degree recoil kick per shot (recovers in 0.25 s) and a slow breathing drift (0.15 degrees); local\n400\tmultiplayer and hub rounds use keyframes only and no kick. Canoe vantage: a gentle bob (z +-4 cm, roll +-0.6 degrees,"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9e576ea7-ae6c-4602-b536-6bfa64a9b891","uuid":"c06256d7-846f-4b92-807e-a98659b07c31","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:38:10.761Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-build--20261104-053803--x4cdb93-work/9e576ea7-ae6c-4602-b536-6bfa64a9b891/tool-results/bttqiyxal.txt","content":"# Big Buck Hunt-Off\n\nBuild plan for the developers who will make this game from `TASK.md` and this file alone. Read `TASK.md` first, then this\nplan top to bottom once; after that, use it as a reference. Everything here is a decision unless it says \"optional\".\nWhere a number is given, it is the starting default (a dev slider where marked), to be tuned in play.\n\n---\n\n## 0. At a glance\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Title** | **Big Buck Hunt-Off** (on screen: `BIG BUCK HUNT-OFF`). Picked as TASK.md asks: 50 riffs on \"Big Buck Hunter Pro\" brainstormed, one drawn with Node's `crypto.randomInt` (it drew #33 of 50). |\n| **Dev subfolder** | `BigBuckHuntOff-BigBuckHunterPro-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174813/` (GameTitle and SOURCE in PascalCase with spaces and punctuation dropped) |\n| **Deliverable** | `BigBuckHuntOff-BigBuckHunterPro-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174813.html` in this folder's root (beside `PLAN.md`), plus `exit-interview.json` beside it |\n| **Genre** | Arcade light-gun hunting shooter (a shooting gallery in a living landscape), with party versus and bonus mini-games |\n| **Perspective** | First person, on rails: a fixed vantage per site with scripted camera pans over a pseudo-3D heightfield landscape. Plus a slow-motion bullet cam on trophy kills, side-on x-ray Shot Chart cards in the tallies, and 2D screens for menus and the Lodge |\n| **Players** | 1 to 4 on one screen (simultaneous, competitive); 1 to 4 online in AI Hub sessions (ghost mode) |\n| **Engine** | my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff package (full engine `my-3d2dge.js`), extended with new modules |\n\n**Pitch.** It's the 41st Annual Big Buck Hunt-Off at Whisker Lake Lodge. You sit in a tree stand, a blind or a canoe and\nlook out over a landscape that lives: deer, elk and moose wander, graze, stop and bolt across creek fords, cornfields,\nalpine meadows and frozen bogs. Pick out the bucks (only the bucks), pump your rifle between shots, and go for clean,\nlong, running shots for big points. Shoot a doe and you sit out the rest of the site. After each site an x-ray Shot\nChart shows exactly where every round went. Each hunt ends with a legendary trophy animal and a silly bonus round, and\nyour total climbs the Hunt-Off standings against the lodge regulars. Friends can play on the same couch with their own\nreticles, or online through AI Hub.\n\n**Core loop (one sentence, put it at the top of the game code):** spot the bucks, shoot clean, pump, never shoot a doe;\nbag them before they're gone, then read your shots in x-ray and climb the standings.\n\n**Session lengths:** Hunt-Off campaign about 17 minutes (3 hunts); a single hunt about 6 minutes; Party Quick Draw\nabout 5 minutes; an AI Hub round 3 minutes; Practice Range about 3 minutes.\n\n### 0.1 Where each TASK.md requirement is covered\n\nThis stage writes the plan only (STAGE.md: \"Don't build it yet: plan it\"). The game's HTML and `exit-interview.json`\nare build-stage outputs; this plan names them, specifies them and gives the exit interview's filled template.\n\n| TASK.md asks for | Covered in |\n|---|---|\n| Genre(s) that fit the source | 0 (genre), 1.2, 2 |\n| Camera perspective(s) | 0 (perspective), 5.1, 5.13 (bullet cam), 5.14 (Shot Chart), 5.18 (Lodge) |\n| Title: a riff on the source's name, 50 brainstormed, one picked by a real RNG | 0 (Big Buck Hunt-Off, #33 of 50 by `crypto.randomInt`) |\n| Research (our choice) | 1.1 (each fact confirmed, reported or unknown), Appendix F |\n| Package: one HTML file with the exact name; downloadable dependencies optional | 0, 3.5 (bundle layout, build checks), 10 |\n| Tech stack on the handoff package; the engine may change; new systems, art, animation | 3.1, 4.1, Appendix C (engine changes) |\n| Art direction: engine's native style, new art for the source's feel, library for generic props | 2, 5.3 (steps 4-5), 5.4, 5.5 |\n| Animation: engine's native system, new animations | 5.4 (Quadruped gaits and poses), 5.5 (Humanoid and Blob poses) |\n| Audio: music, sfx and voices in code; gibberish voices | 5.19 (E.Babble), 5.20 (songs, ambience, sfx) |\n| Online: AI Hub lib/hub | 5.26 (ghost mode), 3.5 (metas, client first), 9.4 (harness) |\n| Working environment: dev subfolder, modules then one bundle in root, CDN allowed, shared machine etiquette, `file://`, server on 127.0.0.1 port 0, stop only own PIDs | 0, 3.2, 3.3, 3.5, 9.4 |\n| Performance budget unlimited (FPS out of scope) | 5.3 (detail, effects, second render pass for scopes), 8 (renderer row) |\n| Testing: headless scripts with `game.advance` and `game.debug`, a scripted playthrough of the whole game, one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changes, screenshots only where scripts can't check, no subagent or workflow reviews | 9.1-9.5, the Tests paragraph of each system in 5, Appendix D (debug handle) |\n| Goal: an original game inspired by the source | 1.2, 1.3, 1.4 |\n| Scope: feature-complete, polished vertical slice showing all features; world, characters, themes, tone; infrastructure to build the rest with designer tools | 5 (all systems), 6 (all content), 4.2 (registries and designer tools), 7 (M6 slice first) |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu: standard tools, live sliders for key variables, reset to defaults | 5.22, Appendix A, Appendix C (3) |\n| Attract mode: 10 s idle on title or menu, AI demo loop, any input exits, may cheat | 5.21, 9.2 (flow/attract) |\n| Multiplayer: AI Hub integration and the game on its own | 5.11 (local 1-4), 5.26 (hub; runs normally when `connect()` answers null) |\n| Narrative tone like the source | 2, 6.8 (story beats), 6.9 (barks) |\n| 10-foot, controller-first UI; DualSense or any standard pad; KB+M native with the genre's keys; mouse aims, clicks and hover-focuses; prompts follow the last device; PlayStation glyphs with an Xbox option; no dense web-style screens | 5.12, 5.17, 5.23, 5.24, Appendix C (2) |\n| Touch-only devices: no touch controls, \"Controller or keyboard required\", the settings button, attract mode runs | 5.25 |\n| Done: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs | 3.5 (build check), 9.2 (unit/lint), 10 |\n| Source Information (every field, \"unknown\" when unconfirmed) in the chat reply and an About & Credits screen | 5.27, Appendix E.1 |\n| Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback answers | 5.27, Appendix E.2 |\n| `exit-interview.json` in the exact shape, beside the HTML, checked to parse | Appendix E.3 (filled template, validated) |\n\n---\n\n## 1. The source: what to capture, what not to copy\n\n### 1.1 What research confirmed about Big Buck Hunter Pro\n\nResearch done while planning, on 2026-11-03: pages read directly were Wikipedia's \"Big Buck Hunter\" article, the\nHighway Games product listing for the Big Buck Hunter Pro cabinet, and Macworld's review of the iPhone port. Giant\nBomb, TV Tropes, MobyGames and the Sinden wiki refused the fetch (HTTP 403) and one retailer page was rate-limited\n(HTTP 429); for those, only search-result summaries were seen. **Confirmed** below means read on a fetched page;\n**reported** means seen only in search summaries; **unknown** means not found. Use \"unknown\" in the reports for\nanything not confirmed.\n\n| Fact | Status | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| Arcade game designed by Play Mechanix, produced and distributed by Raw Thrills; released January 23, 2006 (a retailer database lists 2005) | confirmed | Wikipedia; Highway Games |\n| Two guns on one cabinet; 1-2 players; single, versus and co-operative play, plus a head-to-head \"Shootout\" mode | confirmed | Wikipedia; Highway Games |\n| Shoot the males as they cross the screen; a player who shoots a female is out (the iPhone port: hitting a doe ends the scene, bucks already taken still count) | confirmed | Wikipedia; Macworld |\n| A hunt is split into five screens called treks, and a bonus round ends the session; a round ends when all males are shot or some escape | confirmed | Wikipedia |\n| Points depend on distance, the animal's weight or size, rack points, accuracy and bonus critters | confirmed | Wikipedia; Macworld |\n| A buck dies to one shot in the vitals or two anywhere on the body; after each scene a screen shows where the shots landed (the iPhone port) | confirmed | Macworld |\n| Animals graze until a few seconds pass or the first shot, then bolt; each scene has three bucks, often with a doe (the iPhone port) | confirmed | Macworld |\n| Five huntable animals: whitetail deer, elk, pronghorn antelope, moose, bighorn sheep; hunts organized by species, not region | confirmed | Highway Games |\n| Critters for bonus points: mountain lions, raccoons, possums, skunks, blackbirds, bluebirds, squirrels, foxes, wolves, rabbits | confirmed | Highway Games |\n| 15 bonus rounds, also sold as \"Bonus Only\" (named: Duck Hunt, Pappy's Moonshine, Pie in the Sky, Boars Gone Wild, Mug Shot, Turkey Hunt) | confirmed | Highway Games |\n| Monthly national tournaments | confirmed | Wikipedia |\n| Pump-action guns (\"pump to reload\" speech call) | reported | search summary of a Big Buck Hunter Reloaded change log; the iPhone port reloads after every shot (Macworld, confirmed) |\n| Head shots, heart shots, or \"peppering\" a buck | reported | search summary of Giant Bomb |\n| Tone: playful, jokey (the review's \"Freeze, gopher!\"), rural humor in the bonus round names | confirmed in spirit | Macworld; Highway Games |\n| The announcer's exact lines, the cabinet's art and music | unknown | (not researched on purpose: we don't copy them) |\n\n### 1.2 Distinctive elements, turned into our mechanics\n\n| Source element | Our version (original) |\n|---|---|\n| Two pump guns, head to head | Up to 4 simultaneous reticles, each with its own pump-action rifle; shared animals, first hit takes it |\n| Bucks only, a doe puts you out | Bucks/bulls are targets; does, fawns and calves are protected. Classic rule: shoot one and you're benched for the site (a Casual setting turns it into a points penalty) |\n| Sites (treks) and a bonus round | 3 hunts, each with 3 sites, a legendary **Trophy** encounter and a bonus game, all on one landscape per hunt with walk-in camera moves between vantages |\n| Distance, weight and accuracy scoring | Points = species base (from antler size) × distance × hit zone × running bonus, plus accuracy, clean sweep and critter bonuses |\n| Head and heart shots; a screen showing where shots landed | Real 3D hit zones on every animal (heart, lungs, head, neck, shoulder, gut, ham, legs, antlers), shown afterwards on our own **x-ray Shot Chart** |\n| Critters for bonus points | 12 critter designs across the hunts; some protected (skunk, Bessie the cow, eagle, loons) with comic penalties |\n| Goofy bonus rounds | Crow Patrol, Saucer Season, Snowball Standoff (ours) |\n| Tournaments | The Hunt-Off campaign against rival regulars; AI Hub rounds as an online Hunt-Off |\n| Pump to reload | Pump (rack) after every shot; when the tube is empty, the pump reloads it |\n| Loud announcer | An emcee with short all-caps barks, plus a folksy guide and rivals who talk in Animalese gibberish with captions |\n\n### 1.3 Additions that make it a better game\n- **The call** (grunt, bugle, moose grunt): a limited button that makes nearby animals stop and look for a moment: a\n  skill shot setup, and the key to two trophies.\n- **The scope**: a round magnifying lens around your reticle (works for four players on one screen), with gentle sway\n  and a hold-breath button for long shots across a valley.\n- **Hills, cover and light**: animals vanish behind ridges, trunks and fog banks; dawn mist, dusk backlight, snowfall.\n\n### 1.4 Do not copy\nNo names, logos, characters, art, music, sounds, bonus-round concepts, levels or look from Big Buck Hunter. Our title,\ncast, hunting grounds, bonus games, writing and art are our own. Whitetail deer, elk and moose are real animals every\nhunting game shares (the source also had pronghorn and bighorn sheep); our hunts, sites, trophies and critter roles\nare original, and none of our bonus games matches the source's named rounds. Reticle colors are the AI Hub seat colors (red, blue,\ngreen, gold), not the cabinet's gun colors. No gore: hits throw puffs of fur and dust; animals fold up and lie down.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Look, sound and tone\n\n**Art direction: the engine's native retro-modern pixel style** (its Look target: PS1/N64-era 2D, shaded with\n`E.tones`, outlined pixel art, the 5x7 font, `E.font.title` logos). Internal resolution `res: [480, 270]` with\n`expand: true` (a 16:9 screen shows 480 x 270; wider screens show more landscape). The world is drawn by our own\npseudo-3D renderer (section 5.3) in the engine's pixels: a heightfield landscape a column at a time like the early-90s\nvoxel terrain games, with billboards (trees, rocks, animals) scaled by distance.\n\nNew art made for this game: every animal and critter (procedural rigs), antlers, hunting props (tree stand, ground\nblind frame, canoe bow, paper targets, trap house, scarecrow Hank, snowmen, farm dressing: barn, porch, corn rows),\nthe Lodge, the logo, glyph icons, HUD pieces. Generic props come from the engine's library and asset store\n(pines, broadleaf trees, bushes, rocks, stumps, logs, fences, hay bales, tents, signposts, campfire rings: 5.3,\nstep 4).\n\nPer-hunt palettes:\n- **Hollow Creek** (autumn morning): pale blue sky to peach horizon, low eastern sun, olive and ochre grass, russet\n  leaf litter, orange oaks, red maples, white birches, dark teal creek with white riffles, valley mist.\n- **Bugle Ridge** (alpine dusk): deep blue to orange-pink sky, sun setting behind the peaks (backlit animals with warm\n  rim outlines), gold aspens, dark spruce, grey talus, gold-green meadow, a lake below catching the sunset.\n- **Muskeg Lake** (first snow, overcast): grey-blue sky, falling snow, white and blue-grey ground, black spruce, orange\n  tamaracks, brown reeds and cattails, dark water with ice at the edges, dense far fog.\n\n**Tone and writing**: like the source, short, loud and folksy. The emcee barks in all caps (\"BUCKS ONLY!\", \"HEART\nSHOT!\", \"DOE! YOU'RE OUT!\"); the guide is dry and homespun; the rivals are small-town braggarts. Lines stay under\nabout 60 characters so they read at 10 feet. Sample lines are in 6.8 and 6.9; write the rest in the same voice.\n\n**Audio**: everything synthesized in code (engine chip synth plus small Web Audio graphs). Bluegrass-flavored chiptune\nmusic (banjo-roll pulse arpeggios, fiddle square leads, triangle bass, washboard noise drums), nature ambience, layered\ngunshots with valley echoes, animal calls, Animalese/Simlish gibberish voices. Section 5.20.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Setup, folders and build\n\n### 3.1 Getting the engine and the hub client\nThe plan was written against **engine handoff v0.85.2 (my-3d2dge commit 4390a98)** and **AI Hub lib/hub (ai-hub commit\nf8835e4, session client 1.4.0)**. A copy fetched while planning is at\n`/home/micha/.cache/x2e02c0-plan-research/{engine,hub}` (sparse clones: `engine/dist/handoff/`, `hub/lib/hub/`). Copy\nfrom it, or clone again the same way into a new, empty folder inside the dev subfolder:\n\n```sh\ngit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git vendor/engine-src\ngit -C vendor/engine-src sparse-checkout set dist/handoff\ngit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub.git vendor/hub-src\ngit -C vendor/hub-src sparse-checkout set lib/hub\n```\n\nRead in the handoff: `README.md`, `API.md` (whole), `AI_GUIDE.md` (as needed), `docs/ASSETS.md` (whole, before\ndrawing props), `API-INDEX.md` (grep only), `modules/mode7.js` header (our renderer borrows its view cache and its\nBackdrop-turning trick), `modules/arcade.js` header, `kits/kart.js` (Mode 7 usage), `game/` (template and its test).\nIn the hub: `README.md` (whole), `examples/ghost.html` (the pattern we follow), `harness.html`.\n\n### 3.2 Environment notes (this machine)\n- `/tmp` is a nearly full tmpfs (99-100% used by others). Point scratch space at the dev folder: `export\n  TMPDIR=$PWD/.tmp` before Playwright, Chromium or anything that writes temp files. Never delete other people's files.\n- Node 24 is installed; Playwright's browsers are cached in `~/.cache/ms-playwright` (chromium-1243). `npm install`\n  in `vendor/engine-src/dist/handoff` installs the pinned Playwright 1.63.0 for the engine's tools (if it wants a\n  different Chromium build than the cache has, run `npx playwright install chromium` there).\n- Other agents share this machine, its ports and processes. Prefer `file://` and headless Node. The hub harness needs a\n  server: `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` (it prints the port), record its PID, check the page title is\n  ours before trusting a result, and stop only that PID.\n\n### 3.3 Dev folder layout\n\n```\nBigBuckHuntOff-BigBuckHunterPro-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174813/\n  engine/my-3d2dge.js           the full engine, our copy (changes listed in Appendix C)\n  engine/modules/vista.js       NEW engine module: E.Lens, E.Heightfield, E.Vista (renderer), E.Impostors\n  engine/modules/quadruped.js   NEW engine module: E.Quadruped (four-legged rig) and antler generators\n  engine/modules/babble.js      NEW engine module: E.Babble (gibberish voices) and audio echo helper\n  engine/assets/nature.js       the few store entries we use, cut with tools/asset-set.mjs\n  hub/session.js                AI Hub client 1.4.0, verbatim (never edited)\n  hub/harness.html bridge.js round.js   for the conformance check only (not bundled)\n  src/*.js                      the game, in numbered files (3.4)\n  build/manifest.json           the script order, shared by the build and the tests\n  build/build.mjs               bundles everything into the single HTML in the work root\n  index.html                    the dev page: loads the same scripts as separate files\n  tests/run.mjs                 THE test command (section 9)\n  tests/lib/*.mjs               the headless loader, the hub stub, the bot driver, Playwright helpers\n  tests/unit/*.test.mjs, tests/flow/*.test.mjs, tests/browser/*.test.mjs\n  tools/                        copies of the engine's check.mjs, filmstrip.mjs, replay.mjs (paths fixed up)\n  .tmp/                         scratch (gitignore-style: never bundled)\n```\n\n### 3.4 Game source files (order = load order)\n\n| File | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| `00-core.js` | `const E = My3D2dge`; constants (`M = 16` world units per metre); `def(kind, id, spec)` registries; event bus; RNG streams; `TUNING` (dev settings); `Records` (E.store); the core-loop comment |\n| `01-game.js` | `new E.Game(...)` (options in 5.24), our `E.Shell` with theme, help and game settings, E.Arcade instance |\n| `10-world.js` | World: loads a hunt's Heightfield, props, water, sky, weather; caches up to 2 hunts |\n| `11-props.js` | prop registry, custom prop painters, scatter rules, occluder volumes and their spatial grid |\n| `12-fx.js` | world-space particles, screen-space weather, banners' shake; cosmetic RNG only |\n| `20-species.js` | species presets for E.Quadruped (whitetail, elk, moose, canid, cow), palettes, antler specs |\n| `21-critters.js` | 12 critter designs: rigs (E.Blob or E.Quadruped presets), behaviors, points, penalties |\n| `22-cast.js` | Humanoid cast (Dub, Granny Mae, Dwayne, Loretta, Earl, the Emcee) and their voices |\n| `30-actors.js` | animal and critter runtime: orders, paths on terrain, herds, senses, reactions, wounds, death |\n| `31-patterns.js` | spawn pattern library (cross, emerge, graze, herdPass, ridgeRun, wade, bolt, charge, decoyDoe) |\n| `32-site.js` | Site runtime: script runner, camera director, end rules, per-site results |\n| `40-weapon.js` | rifle (pump, tube, reload), scope (lens, sway, breath), call |\n| `41-aim.js` | reticle control per device, aim assist |\n| `42-shot.js` | shot resolution (rays against zones, occluders, terrain, water), impacts, spook events |\n| `43-score.js` | scoring, bonuses, penalties, stats, stars, ranks, rivals |\n| `50-players.js` | player slots: device, color and reticle shape, weapon, score, benching; joining |\n| `51-hud.js` | HUD, banners, score pops, captions, prompts |\n| `52-glyphs.js` | PlayStation, Xbox, keyboard and mouse glyph icons; prompt labels by last device |\n| `53-ui.js` | UIKit: focus lists and grids, cards, mouse hover focus, clicks, back, UI sounds |\n| `60-tally.js` | site tally and x-ray Shot Chart |\n| `61-trophy.js` | trophy encounters and the Trophy Cam |\n| `62-bonus.js` | bonus game framework and the three bonus games |\n| `63-range.js` | Practice Range stations |\n| `70-audio.js` | songs, sfx definitions, ambience beds, gunshot echoes |\n| `71-barks.js` | barks and captions; dialog scenes with portraits and Babble voices |\n| `80-hollow.js`, `81-bugle.js`, `82-muskeg.js`, `83-range-map.js` | each hunt's map, vantages, sites, trophy, bonus |\n| `85-story.js` | story beats, rivals' scores, ceremony |\n| `90-title.js`, `91-menu.js`, `92-hunt.js`, `93-lodge.js`, `94-about.js` | scenes |\n| `95-attract.js` | attract director and HunterBot |\n| `96-dev.js` | dev mode: tuning rows, tools, debug overlays, free camera, Animal Lab, Sound Lab |\n| `97-hub.js` | AI Hub ghost-mode integration |\n| `99-start.js` | `game.debug`, `game.stateHash`, deep links, boot steps, `game.start` |\n\n### 3.5 The bundle\n`node build/build.mjs` writes `../BigBuckHuntOff-BigBuckHunterPro-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174813.html`:\n\n```html\n<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<script>/* hub/session.js, byte for byte */</script>\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\">\n<title>Big Buck Hunt-Off</title>\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"3\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Most points in 3 minutes wins. Bucks only, never does!\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Aim: mouse or left stick · Shoot: click or R2 · Pump: R or Square\">\n<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\"><meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<style>html,body{margin:0;height:100%;background:#000;overflow:hidden}#screen{width:100%;height:100%;display:block;image-rendering:pixelated;touch-action:none;cursor:none}</style>\n</head><body>\n<!-- E.bootSplash('BIG BUCK HUNT-OFF') markup -->\n<canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas>\n<script>/* engine/my-3d2dge.js */</script>\n<script>/* engine/modules/vista.js, quadruped.js, babble.js; engine/assets/nature.js */</script>\n<script>/* src/*.js in manifest order, each in its own <script> or concatenated inside one IIFE per file */</script>\n</body></html>\n```\n\nThe build asserts: the session client is the first script right after `<meta charset>` and matches `hub/session.js`\nexactly (hash); every hub meta is present; goal is at most 60 characters and howto at most 80; no `TODO`, `FIXME`,\n`XXX`, `placeholder` or `lorem` anywhere in `src/` or the new modules; no `fetch(`, `WebSocket`, `RTCPeerConnection`,\n`alert(`, `confirm(`, `prompt(` or `window.open` in game code. It runs from `file://` with no network. No CDN is needed.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Architecture\n\n### 4.1 The stack\n\n```\n AI Hub client (session.js)  ─ first script; inert outside a session\n my-3D2dge full engine       ─ game loop (fixed 1/120 s steps), scenes, input (keys, mouse, pads, players),\n                               standard menus (E.Shell HTML: pause, settings, controls, help), settings, saves,\n                               E.store, audio synth + music clock, E.Arcade (tables, initials), E.Dialog,\n                               E.Humanoid, E.Blob, E.Backdrop, E.View, px/font/ui drawing, tweens, scripts, paths\n NEW engine modules          ─ E.Lens, E.Heightfield, E.Vista, E.Impostors   (vista.js)\n                               E.Quadruped, E.Antlers                         (quadruped.js)\n                               E.Babble, audio echo send                      (babble.js)\n Game (src/)                 ─ content registries, actors and AI, sites, weapons, shots, scoring, players,\n                               HUD/UI, modes and scenes, attract, dev mode, hub link\n```\n\nWe do not use `E.Mode7` itself (its floor is flat); `vista.js` reuses its ideas: `view(k)` caching for drawing rigs at a\ndistance's scale, and drawing a Backdrop through a stand-in renderer whose `ix` turns with the heading.\n\n### 4.2 Conventions\n- **Units**: 16 world units = 1 m (`M = 16`). x east, y south, z up (the engine's). Content data is written in\n  metres and degrees and converted on load.\n- **Angles**: yaw 0 = east (+x), pi/2 = south (+y). Site data uses bearings in degrees relative to the site's base\n  yaw (negative = left of centre) and distances in metres: `[bearing, dist]`.\n- **Time**: everything in seconds of game time; `update(dt)` only (fixed steps). Gameplay never reads `performance.now`.\n- **Randomness**: never `Math.random` in gameplay. `RNG.stream(name)` returns `E.rng(seed)` generators: `world`\n  (map generation: seed per hunt, always the same map), `spawn` (site variations: seed per run), `ai` (per animal,\n  seeded from the run seed and the animal's id), `bot`, and `fx` (cosmetic only; may be drawn from freely, so its\n  draws never shift the others). In an AI Hub session the run seed comes from `AIHub.rng('hunt')`. A test greps `src/`\n  and the new modules for `Math.random` (there should be none).\n- **Registries** (`def(kind, id, spec)`): `species`, `critter`, `hunt`, `site`, `trophy`, `bonus`, `pattern`, `prop`,\n  `bark`, `voice`, `song`, `beat` (story). Content refers to other content by id and never edits core code. This is\n  what lets the rest of the game be built out later with designer tools: a new hunt, site, species, critter or bonus\n  game is data (plus at most a small painter), authored with the dev free camera's position and bearing readout and\n  previewed in the Animal Lab and Sound Lab.\n- **Event bus**: `bus.on(name, fn)`, `bus.emit(name, data)`. Events: `shot`, `hit`, `kill`, `wound`, `miss`, `doe`,\n  `critter`, `penalty`, `spook`, `escape`, `lost`, `siteStart`, `siteEnd`, `trophySpotted`, `bark`, `pump`, `reload`,\n  `call`. HUD, barks, audio, stats, the bot and the hub link listen; gameplay code emits.\n- **Determinism**: the same seed and inputs give the same game (the engine's fixed step plus our streams).\n  `game.stateHash = () => [every actor's x, y, state, hp, every player's score, rounds]`.\n\n### 4.3 Scenes and flow\n\nEngine scenes (`game.go(name, data, { fade })`):\n\n| Scene | pausable | What |\n|---|---|---|\n| `title` | no | live Hollow Creek vista behind the logo; PRESS START; idle 10 s starts the attract mode (5.21) |\n| `attract` | no | the demo playlist; any input returns to `title` |\n| `menu` | no | main menu and its sub-pages (one scene, a page stack): Hunt-Off, Single Hunt, Party Hunt, Bonus Games, Practice Range, The Lodge, Settings, About & Credits. Idle 10 s on a top-level page starts the attract mode too |\n| `hunt` | **yes** | all gameplay: a hunt's segments (below), the Range, bonus-only and party modes |\n| `lodge` | no | the trophy room (side view, engine props and lights) |\n| `about` | no | About & Credits pages (5.27) |\n| `hub` | no (pausing is off in sessions) | the AI Hub round: own card, the timed Hub Hunt-Off, final standings |\n| `animallab`, `soundlab` | no | dev-only labs (reachable from dev mode and deep links) |\n\nSegments inside `hunt` (one scene, so the loaded world, timers and particles survive between them):\n\n```\nhuntIntro (establishing pan, hunt card, briefing dialog)\n  -> site 1: siteIntro -> play -> siteEnd -> tally -> walk (camera walks the trail to the next vantage)\n  -> site 2 ...  -> site 3 ...\n  -> trophyBrief (campfire legend dialog) -> trophy (play) -> trophyEnd (Trophy Cam / \"the one that got away\") -> tally\n  -> bonusBrief (host dialog) -> bonus (play) -> bonusTally\n  -> huntResults (total, rank, rivals, initials via E.Arcade) -> outro dialog\n  -> next hunt (Hunt-Off campaign: autosave, then load the next world) or back to the menu\n```\n\nPause menu items (`menu: { items }`): Restart site, Restart hunt, Dev mode. Standard: Resume, Settings, Controls, Full\nscreen, Help, Quit to title (`quit: 'title'`).\n\n### 4.4 The frame\n\n`update(dt)` (each fixed step), in this order: input and reticles -> weapons (pump timers, scope, breath, calls) ->\nshots fired this step are resolved -> site script and spawns -> actors (AI, movement, gaits, rigs) -> director camera ->\nfx -> HUD state, barks, audio cues -> hub link (session only).\n\n`draw(r)`: `vista.draw(r, lens)` as one `r.overlay`: sky and Backdrop, terrain raster, billboards far to near (props,\nanimals, critters, world particles), foreground dressing, weather, fog and grade, then the scope lenses, reticles,\nscore pops; then the HUD overlay. Engine lighting, the engine camera and engine particles are not used in Vista scenes\n(the overlay is unlit and screen-space); the Lodge uses the engine's normal side view, props and lights.\n\n### 4.5 The test handle\n`game.debug` (Appendix D): `snapshot()`, `aimAt(p, actorId, zone)`, `fire(p)`, `pump(p)`, `scope(p, on)`,\n`call(p)`, `goto(huntId, segment)`, `spawn(kind, opts)`, `skip()`, `bagAll()`, `bot(on, opts)`, `seed(n)`,\n`tuning(key, value)`. Tests, `tools/check.mjs` and the bot use it.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Systems\n\nEach system lists what to build, its numbers and how to test it (test names refer to section 9).\n\n### 5.1 Lens and the camera director (`vista.js`: `E.Lens`; `32-site.js`: Director)\n\n**Projection** (a sheared perspective: verticals stay vertical, which suits billboards and the column renderer):\n- Camera `{ x, y, z, yaw, pitch, fov }` (fov = degrees across the screen's height; pitch + = looking down).\n- Screen `W x H`, centre `cx = W/2`, `cy = H/2`; focal `f = (H/2) / tan(fov/2)`; `horizonY = cy - f * tan(pitch)`.\n- Forward `F = (cos yaw, sin yaw)`, right `R = (-sin yaw, cos yaw)`.\n- `project(P)`: `d = P - C`; `D = d.F` (forward distance); `L = d.R`; null if `D < 1 m`; `k = f / D` (pixels per unit);\n  `sx = cx + L*k`; `sy = horizonY - (P.z - C.z)*k`. Returns `[sx, sy, k, D]`.\n- `ray(sx, sy)`: direction `dir = F + R*((sx - cx)/f) + up*((horizonY - sy)/f)`; the point at forward distance D is\n  `C + D*dir`. Shots, picking and the bot all use this.\n- **Magnifying about a point** (the scope lens): the same camera with focal `f*mag`, centred so screen point `s0` lands\n  on the lens centre: `s' = c_lens + (s - s0)*mag`. The lens is an exact zoom of the main view, so the shot through its\n  centre is the main view's ray through `s0`.\n- **Rigs and props** are drawn as orthographic billboards: an `E.View('vista', 'Vista', yawDeg, pitchDeg, k, 1)` with\n  `yawDeg = -yaw*180/pi - 90` (Mode 7's formula) and `pitchDeg` = the angle from the camera down to the object,\n  clamped to 4..30 degrees, quantized to 2 degrees; views cached by (k in 1/32 steps below 1, 1/16 above; yaw in 2\n  degree steps; pitch).\n\n**Director** (per site, deterministic; keyed to site time, never to player input, so all players and all AI Hub copies\nsee the same frame): keyframes `[t, { bearing, pitch, fov, ease, dur }]`; optional `follow` mode in single player\n(yaw eases toward the centroid of live bucks, clamped to the site's bearing range, smoothed over 0.8 s). Single player\nalso gets a 0.4 degree recoil kick per shot (recovers in 0.25 s) and a slow breathing drift (0.15 degrees); local\nmultiplayer and hub rounds use keyframes only and no kick. Canoe vantage: a gentle bob (z +-4 cm, roll +-0.6 degrees,","numLines":400,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1572}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01XgGTyvrxT3CnewrCrHx99F","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
