{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghx4PjDQx2qN6twbPQBK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Big Buck Hunt-Off is built as one HTML file in the work folder root, and `exit-interview.json` sits beside it. The headless suite passes all 115 checks, including a scripted full Hunt-Off with a cheating bot, and the built file passed the AI Hub conformance harness. I never ran the game on a real controller, so gamepad aiming and pad prompts are untested beyond the code reading the engine's pad state.\n\nThe file is `BigBuckHuntOff-BigBuckHunterPro-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-053803.html`. The title and Hollow Creek sites rendered fine in a screenshot run, and a drawn play-through of the Hunt-Off in the built file ran with no engine errors. I followed TASK.md for the file name, since PLAN.md names an Opus-dated one.\n\nIt is a first-person arcade hunting shooter on the my-3D2dge engine, with its own pseudo-3D landscape renderer for the world. It has nine hunting sites, three trophy hunts, three bonus games, Uncle Dub's practice range, a trophy lodge, and an x-ray shot chart after each site. Dev mode (sliders with reset, tools, overlays, free camera, Animal Lab, Sound Lab) opens from the pause menu. After 10 seconds idle on the title or menu, the attract mode starts and any input exits it. It plays one to four on one screen (extra seats can be bots). In an AI Hub session it runs a timed 3-minute Hunt-Off.\n\nTwo things in this build are rougher than the plan: animals are small and often hidden behind reeds at distance, and ground colour patches are blocky. Both are playable but unpolished.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:38:03\n- **Source:** Big Buck Hunter Pro (Play Mechanix / Raw Thrills arcade shooter, 2006)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md (written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort)\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade light-gun hunting shooter with party versus and bonus mini-games. First person, on rails: a fixed vantage per site with scripted camera pans over a pseudo-3D column landscape, plus side-on x-ray shot-chart cards and 2D menu and lodge screens.\n- **Tech:** JavaScript in one HTML file; my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (handoff package, unmodified); own pseudo-3D strip renderer; AI Hub session client 1.4.0; headless Node vm tests.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's retro-modern outlined pixel style at 480x270. All animals, antlers, props, scopes and HUD are drawn procedurally in code.\n- **Animation:** Engine pixel drawing plus a procedural four-legged rig (two-bone leg IK, walk/trot/run gaits, graze/alert/drink poses, death fold). Engine Humanoid portraits for the cast.\n- **Audio:** All music, sound effects and voices are synthesised in code with the engine's chip synth: bluegrass-flavoured chiptune, layered gunshots with valley echoes, animal calls, Animalese-style gibberish voices. No audio files.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode. It has its own card, a 3-minute round, ghost reticles for other players, and progress and result reporting. It plays normally when no hub answers.\n- **Testing:** Headless scripted tests with `game.advance` and `game.debug` (one command, `node tests/run.mjs`, failures plus a one-line count), plus a browser smoke run of the built file. No subagents or workflows.\n- **Research:** None this build. The plan's research was used as given and not re-verified.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 13 real issues. The main ones were a Big Mo crash every frame, a hub-mode crash on start, magenta ground and trees from `rgb()` colour strings, and a Trophy Cam that fired on every dev-spawned kill. The rest included a bot that fired 74 shots for one hit, a trophy the bot could never trigger, flying critters climbing off screen, banner spam after a lost trophy, unknown input actions in dev mode, and font glyphs that printed as `?`.\n- **How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?** Three: the Big Mo update crash, the AI Hub round crash on start, and the magenta rendering.\n- **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The scripted full Hunt-Off playthrough with a cheating expert bot, which confirmed through every dialog and tally across all 15 sites and a ceremony. I also ran it in the built file with drawing on, which exercised the draw paths headless runs skip.\n- **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The determinism hash and the 160-second attract survival check never failed. The multiplier unit checks mostly caught mistakes in my own test helpers.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most about the engine?** The fixed-step loop with `game.advance` and headless mode, so the whole game could be played and checked in Node in seconds. Also the chip synth, `E.Dialog` with live portraits, and the free pause, settings and controls screens.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes. `px` colours and `E.tones` want `#rrggbb`: an `rgb()` string made them return NaN and the ground drew magenta with no error. Input actions must be declared up front. The overlay draw is not run headless, so draw bugs only showed in the browser.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The first-person view: a column terrain renderer with depth strips, billboards, fog, scope lens passes and occlusion-correct shot picking. Also the four-legged animal rig, the animal AI and the bot.\n- **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Fitting a pseudo-3D first-person shooter into a 2D engine built for ground, side and iso views. It has no camera model for it, so the world, hit testing and depth sorting are the game's own.\n- **What did you change in the engine, and why?** None. The engine file is the handoff copy unmodified.\n- **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** A first-person pseudo-3D view type (heightfield, billboards, fog, mouse picking). Accepting CSS colour strings in `px` and `E.tones` would also have saved an hour.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most about this prompt?** The clear deliverable list, the freedom to cut the plan, and the steer to test headless with `game.advance` and `game.debug`.\n- **What was confusing?** The plan names a different output file and dev folder (Opus, 2026-11-03) than TASK.md and STAGE.md ask for. The plan is also far larger than a medium-effort session can build, and it doesn't say which parts are essential.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** The scope: 27 systems, three hunts, trophies, bonus games, a range, dev mode, attract mode, local four-player and an AI Hub round, all in one session with a 10-foot controller-first UI.\n- **What would you change in this prompt?** Mark essential systems against optional ones in a plan, state how much work a build stage should expect, name one output file across all the documents, and say how to count usage so that line isn't always \"unknown\"."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":399,"cache_read_input_tokens":409653,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":399},"output_tokens":788,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9e576ea7-ae6c-4602-b536-6bfa64a9b891","uuid":"84d48215-d77d-48c7-833d-608bd8cd0cb2","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:15:55.453Z","request_id":"req_011Cghx4PP92nSCEP1KBwEAu"}
